# strip-heredocs-failsafe.manifest — the files `th_strip_heredocs` CANNOT strip.
#
# WHAT THIS RECORDS. `th_strip_heredocs` (your-org/nexus-code#806) gives corpus
# lints a source view with heredoc bodies blanked. When it finds a heredoc still
# OPEN at end of file it concludes it has mis-parsed, discards its own output,
# emits the file UNSTRIPPED, and says so on stderr. That fail-safe is the reason
# a stripper bug over-reports instead of going silently blind.
#
# A file listed here is one the fail-safe currently catches. Its consumers fall
# back to raw-source scanning for that file — today's pre-#806 behaviour, loudly.
#
# WHY IT IS PINNED AS DATA. The fail-safe makes a stripper regression HARMLESS,
# not INVISIBLE — and those are different. `#822` found that breaking the
# arithmetic left-shift guard (`$(( budget << streak ))` read as a heredoc
# delimiter) reddened NOTHING, because the fail-safe silently absorbed it: the
# population simply moved 1 → 2 and no assertion looked. This file is what makes
# that movement visible. It is the assertion that would have caught it.
#
# EXACT SET, not a ceiling. A file appearing here that is not listed means a
# parse newly fails; a listed file that no longer appears means one was fixed
# and the line must go. Both directions red, for the same reason the sibling
# manifests do: a list that only grows stops describing the tree.
#
# DO NOT regenerate this to make a red go away. A new entry means
# `th_strip_heredocs` cannot parse something it used to, and the question is
# which guard stopped working.
#
# Checked by monitor/watcher/test-strip-heredocs.sh
#
# <path>::<why this file cannot be parsed>
monitor/watcher/test-assert-shims-wrapped.sh::a multi-line single-quoted awk program containing `<<LAUNCHER` inside a regex; per-line quote state cannot span the quoted string, so the scanner reads it as a heredoc opener whose terminator never arrives
